+## Core-change verification tiers
+
+Name the tier explicitly; no command guesses package scope or escalates into a
+slower build:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh targeted --package codex-core
+bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh workspace
+bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh executable --confirm-slow
+bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh publish --confirm-slow
+```
+
+T1 `targeted` accepts repeatable exact Cargo workspace package names validated
+through `cargo metadata`; if the affected package set is uncertain, use T2
+instead of passing a file or directory. T2 `workspace` is the mandatory
+pre-commit and rebase Phase 5a hard gate even after T1 succeeds. T1/T2 provide
+compile validation only; they do not produce or refresh runnable executables.
+Current evidence is 43.462s for a targeted core edit and 131.889s for a
+high-fanout workspace edit. A 32.791s no-op proved zero rebuilt packages and
+zero compiler requests; it has no arbitrary 30-second wall gate. T3
+`executable` builds both runnable binaries and is approximately 31 minutes
+after a warm changed-core edit. T4 `publish` preserves the four-binary thin-LTO
+release build and is approximately 58 minutes historically, not <=10 minutes.
+The historical 45-68 second result is a link-only floor, not a changed-core
+rebuild. Use `--print-plan-json` to inspect exact T3/T4 argv without running
+them. This task makes routine compile verification fast; it does not make a
+runnable build fast. `codex-fast-runnable-dev-build-tier` owns that separate
+gap.
+
+A cold fingerprint or expected downstream invalidation is not evidence of
+cache corruption. Preserve a healthy shared cache; profile experiments use a
+separate target/cache namespace. Wipe only after a reproducible cache
+read/write/timeout/metadata error. Recorded evidence:
+[`docs/implementation/build-perf-baseline.json`](docs/implementation/build-perf-baseline.json).
