+CI mapping: `.github/workflows/invariant-check.yml` is a non-publishing audit-and-test workflow on
+both `ubuntu-latest` and `windows-latest`. From the wrapper root it runs the static invariant audits
+(`audit_invariants.sh`), the submodule-lag regression test, the fast Node verifier tests, the shell
+sccache-path tests, and the PowerShell measurement/probe tests. It then runs
+`cargo test --workspace` from `external/repos/codex-patched/codex-rs` with no `--lib`, covering
+in-tree unit, in-tree integration, overlay, and Windows-conditional Rust tests. It does not build or
+publish release artifacts.
+
+**Local vs CI split.** Use the wrapper-owned verification tiers rather than invoking an ad hoc
+workspace check:
+
+```bash
+# T1: fast iteration; repeat --package for every exact affected workspace package.
+bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh targeted --package codex-core
+
+# T2: mandatory pre-commit and rebase Phase 5a gate, even after T1 succeeds.
+bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh workspace
+```
+
+T1 validates exact package names through Cargo metadata and never guesses scope from paths or diffs;
+when scope is uncertain, use T2. Scoped `cargo test -p <crate>` remains appropriate for tests
+actually touched. Do not run `cargo test --workspace` locally as routine verification: historically
+it has taken 90+ minutes in the LLVM clang-cl + lld-link + xwin environment and has surfaced
+unrelated rebase debt and Windows-only tracing-test stack overflows. The Linux/Windows workflow is
+the intended full Rust-test signal. As of 2026-04-07 that signal is unavailable because the
+`gim-home` organization policy rejects workflow dispatch with `startup_failure` and no jobs; until
+the tracked `escalate-gim-home-actions-policy` block is lifted, rebase Phase 5b uses its documented
+local interim closeout (green T2, T4, and pre-tag runtime smoke).
+
+T1/T2 are compile-validation tiers only; neither produces or refreshes a runnable executable. Their
+purpose is to replace routine approximately 31-minute optimized-build verification, not to make that
+build fast. The separate `codex-fast-runnable-dev-build-tier` task owns the fast-runnable gap.
+
+T3 is deliberately opt-in:
+`bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh executable --confirm-slow` builds both runnable binaries and
+takes approximately 31 minutes after a warm changed-core edit. It is not the T2 pre-commit/rebase
+gate. T4, `bash scripts/verify-core-change.sh publish --confirm-slow`, preserves the four-binary
+thin-LTO release build and belongs only in the explicit publish/rebase release path; its historical
+changed-core measurement is approximately 58 minutes. The historical 45-68 second result is only a
+link-only floor, not a changed-core rebuild. Use `--print-plan-json` to inspect T3/T4 argv without
+running them. See project `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.override.md` for the operator-facing policy.
 
